md("""# Part 1: Foundations & Baseline — Vector-Based Framing-Effect Analysis

## 1. Analysis objective, hypotheses, and the core narrative

This notebook (Part 1 of 2) tests whether **perceived author identity changes emotion
interpretation** — in humans and in large language models — by representing every annotation as
a full **11-dimensional emotion probability distribution** rather than collapsing it to a single
majority label. Part 2 (`02_persona_effects_and_conclusions.ipynb`) covers the persona-effect
analysis, human-LLM shift alignment, statistical hypothesis tests, qualitative case studies, and
the final conclusions.

**The argument this pair of notebooks makes, in one paragraph:** original human judgments on
these texts contain meaningful disagreement; collapsing that disagreement to a majority label
discards it; representing it as a distribution instead preserves it; perceived authorship
(persona framing) then measurably moves those distributions; the *size* of that movement differs
by text category; and this lets LLMs be evaluated not merely on whether their single answer
matches a human's single answer, but on whether they reproduce the *direction* and *magnitude* of
the human framing-induced change. Nothing below is meant to prove this story — it is meant to
test each link in it against the actual data, honestly reporting where the evidence is strong,
mixed, or absent.

**Exact hypothesis wording**, recovered verbatim from `Desktop/ThesisProposal.pdf` (see
`interpretation/hypotheses_source.md` for the full recovery process and precision notes):

- **RQ1 / H1 (Human Framing):** Does perceived author identity significantly influence human
  emotion judgments of ambiguous texts? *Human emotion judgments will differ significantly across
  author-framing conditions for ambiguous texts, but not (or substantially less) for unambiguous
  control texts.*
- **RQ2 / H2 (Model Framing):** Does perceived author identity significantly influence emotion
  classifications produced by LLMs? *LLM emotion classifications will shift across author-framing
  conditions, indicating sensitivity to minimal author cues.*
- **H3 (Ambiguity Sensitivity):** *Framing effects will be stronger for author-relevant ambiguous
  texts than for author-independent ambiguous texts.*
- **RQ3 / H4 (Alignment):** Do LLMs exhibit framing effects similar in direction and magnitude to
  humans? *The direction of framing-induced shifts in LLM classifications will partially align
  with human judgment shifts, but the magnitude of these shifts will differ.*

No formal, numbered hypothesis about prompt structure exists in the thesis proposal — any
analysis of prompt design in these notebooks is exploratory.

**How results are labeled throughout both notebooks:**
- **[PRIMARY]** — a test that directly operationalizes one of the 4 hypotheses above.
- **[SECONDARY]** — a planned, non-hypothesis descriptive or robustness analysis (e.g. the LLM
  side of the category comparison, per-emotion breakdowns).
- **[EXPLORATORY]** — anything not planned in the original proposal (prompt-structure effects,
  per-model comparisons, qualitative case patterns). Exploratory findings are reported honestly
  but are never treated as if they were confirmatory.

> **How to read this notebook:** every quantitative claim is backed by a specific number computed
> in a cell directly above or below it. Boxed **"Key finding"** callouts summarize what the
> immediately preceding output shows — they do not introduce new claims.""")

md("""### 1.1 Three human data sources — kept separate throughout

This study involves **three distinct human-judgment sources** that must never be conflated. The
table below is the map used consistently across both notebooks.

| Source | Raters/text | Collected when | Role in this analysis |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Original corpus** (crowd-enVent, Troiano et al.) | 5 | Pre-existing, before this study | The **original reference distribution** — how this text was independently rated *before* any of this study's framing manipulation existed. Used to motivate vectorization (Section 3) and as an external validity check (Section 4), never as a persona-effect baseline. |
| **New neutral** (this study) | 3 (occasionally 4-7) | Collected for this study, no author framing | The **experimental "no persona" condition** — the actual baseline every persona-effect comparison in Part 2 is measured against, because it shares the rater pool, era, and task framing with the persona conditions. |
| **New persona A / B** (this study) | 3 (occasionally more) | Collected for this study, WITH author framing | The **experimental framing conditions**. Compared against New neutral (never against the original corpus) to measure the persona effect. |

The original corpus and the new neutral round are **two independent measurements of the same
underlying "no persona" condition**, collected years apart by different rater pools. Section 4
uses their disagreement as a noise-floor check; it is never used as if it were a persona effect.""")

